{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>יִפְקֹד ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי הָרוּחוֹת</b> etc. One must know: (a) why he chose this epithet from among all the epithets of exaltation and praise known for HaShem. (b) Why he lengthened the expression <b>אֲשֶׁר יֵצֵא</b> etc. <b>וַאֲשֶׁר יָבֹא וַאֲשֶׁר יוֹצִיאֵם וַאֲשֶׁר יְבִיאֵם</b>. (c) His saying <b>וְלֹא תִהְיֶה עֲדַת</b> etc. — from where would it arise in the mind that HaShem would conceal His eye from His children so that they would be ״כַּצֹּאן אֲשֶׁר״ etc.?" "Now the matter will be explained along this path: Know that the quality of the intellect — its choosing and its negating — is far removed from the category of equality in the multiplicity of bearers; and just as created beings differ in their form and their stature and there is no equality among them, so too and even more so is it regarding the variation of what is chosen and what is negated in the aspect of intellection and will, in addition to the variation of the particulars of nature in the quality of the composition that is in the elements and the hylic forces from which choice is born in their like. And sometimes equality is found in the disposition and it is hidden — and this is what the Sages, their memory for a blessing, intended (Nedarim 39b) to say ״בֶּן גִּילוֹ״ in the matter of visiting the sick. From this you will understand that it is negated within the category of existent beings that many would be equal to one another, like the six hundred thousand who were in the generation of Moses — and the king whom HaShem sets over them would not be equal to them in their will toward him, except for the man Moses, of whom the Sages, their memory for a blessing, said (Tikkunei Zohar 69) that he was the root of all the souls of the wilderness generation, in the sod of His saying (Isaiah 63:11) ״מֹשֶׁה עַמּוֹ״; and see what I have written on the verse (Numbers 11:12) ״הֶאָנֹכִי הָרִיתִי״ etc. And this was the cause that the one who appoints and the ones appointed were blended into the category of equality, and each one had his heart's content." "<b>And this</b> is also the reason that HaShem commanded appointing judges for your tribes etc. — for the tribe draws near in disposition to its branches. And this Moses, peace be upon him, knew and examined, and he gave an answer to the decree of death: <b>יִפְקֹד ה׳</b> — meaning, let Him designate; <b>אֵל אֱלֹהֵי הָרוּחוֹת</b> — meaning, who knows the spirit of all flesh and the quality of their disposition — let Him designate one whose nature will be equal in disposition to the entire congregation, so that he will agree to what they say and they will agree to what he says to them. And this is what is meant by <b>אֲשֶׁר יֵצֵא לִפְנֵיהֶם</b> — meaning, that he will have the will for them; <b>וַאֲשֶׁר יוֹצִיאֵם</b> etc. — that they will have will like his to go out and come in with him. And he made the matter depend on Him, for He is the King, and a choice such as this is one whose grasp is absolute, beyond anything else, may He be blessed, for He alone knows the substance of the spirits and can know a spirit that will blend with all of them. And a man such as this is not to be found unless he be a man like Moses, of whom all Israel are his branches and he is the root for them all; and apart from this, the congregation of HaShem will be ״כַּצֹּאן אֲשֶׁר אֵין לָהֶם רוֹעֶה״. And the ultimate intent of Moses' words to HaShem is along this path: I will do as my Master speaks, but let this matter first be set right if it can enter the category of proper ordering — and if not, ״תִּהְיֶה עֲדַת ה׳ כַּצֹּאן״ etc. And what emerges from this is ״וְלֹא יָמוּת,״ as alluded to in the word ״לֵאמֹר״ that we have spoken of; and from within the matters you learn that if such a man is found, behold, he too agrees to be gathered to his people, as His saying, may He be blessed."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {2 "The closing phrase והיוצא מזה הוא ולא ימות ('and what emerges from this is and he shall not die') is textually difficult; it appears to mean that finding such a leader resolves the threat of death, hyper-literally rendered here. The reference to the word לאמר ('that we have spoken of') apparently points back to a discussion in an earlier section of the commentary not included in the given text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:50.561899239Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}